---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const b = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const protos = [
 ['Prototype 1', 'Cardboard chassis, DC motors on an L298N driver, four AA cells. Built only to validate driving and motor behaviour.', 'proto1'],
 ['Prototype 2', 'A two-layer layout with the ultrasonic sensor, IMU and Jetson Nano. The extra mass made turns unstable, and sharing one powerbank between motors and compute caused current transients that disturbed the Jetson.', 'proto2'],
 ['Prototype 3', 'A 3D-printed H-shaped skeleton with motor bays and printed D-type shaft adapters, a foam layer that damps vibration and carries the powerbank and Jetson, and a top layer with the locked cargo box.', 'proto3'],
];
---
<Base title="Robotics" description="Robotics projects of Emre Ertürk: the P.C.B indoor delivery robot and PartyBot.">
<div class="wrap pagehead"><span class="kicker">Robotics</span><h1>Robotics</h1><p class="lead">Two course projects in mobile robotics, each built by a team of four in spring 2026.</p></div>

<div class="wrap section" id="pcb" style="padding-top:10px"><hr class="rule"><div class="grid2" style="margin-top:26px">
 <div class="prose rv"><span class="kicker">CENG 483 · 2026 · with H. N. Kömürcü, D. Karabacak, T. İyiarı</span><h2 class="h2" style="margin-top:6px">P.C.B: corridor navigation and secure delivery</h2>
  <p>An indoor robot that carries an item between two landmarks: it drives down a corridor to a target, waits while a user unlocks a keypad-secured cargo box, then turns and returns to base. The design goal was a cheap, reusable platform built from widely available parts.</p>
  <p><b>Architecture.</b> An Arduino Mega runs real-time control: the MPU-6050 IMU, the ultrasonic sensor, two MG995 servos (head scan and cargo lock), the L298N motor driver, a keypad and an LCD. A Jetson Nano runs the mission: it reads a Raspberry Pi camera, detects ArUco markers with OpenCV and sends serial commands to the Arduino.</p>
  <p><b>Straight-line control.</b> On start-up the robot runs an 8 s calibration. For 3 s it estimates the gyroscope’s Z-axis bias at rest. For 5 s it drives open-loop straight and learns a bounded motor-bias term that compensates mismatched motors. A gentle PID correction on the integrated heading then keeps it straight.</p>
  <p><b>Reactive navigation.</b> If an obstacle appears within 200 cm, the robot stops and waits up to 10 s for it to clear before acting, which avoids needless detours around people. If the obstacle stays, a servo-scanned ultrasonic look-around chooses a way out, preferring the right, which gave consistent behaviour in L-shaped corridors.</p>
  <p><b>Landmarks instead of learning.</b> Our first plan used camera-based learning, but small datasets made it unreliable. ArUco markers at the base and target gave dependable start and stop triggers without full SLAM.</p>
  <div class="cta"><a class="btn" href="https://github.com/EmreErturk-3/robotics/blob/main/pcb-delivery-robot/report.pdf">Paper (PDF)</a><a class="btn" href="https://youtube.com/playlist?list=PLwDQJAClSxDNaZMpJzPJgvE02jZJuD9hk">Test videos (YouTube)</a><a class="btn" href="https://github.com/EmreErturk-3/robotics/tree/main/pcb-delivery-robot">Repository</a></div>
  <!-- TODO(Emre): optionally add a line on which parts you personally built (e.g. calibration, navigation, Jetson side). -->
 </div>
 <div class="rv"><figure class="fig"><video src={`${b}/media/robotics/pcb.mp4`} poster={`${b}/media/robotics/pcb_photo.webp`} controls preload="none" playsinline></video><figcaption>Corridor test run of the final prototype.</figcaption></figure></div>
</div>
<h3 class="sub rv" style="margin-top:36px">Three prototypes</h3>
<div class="protos">{protos.map(([h,p,img])=><figure class="rv"><img src={`${b}/media/robotics/${img}.webp`} alt={h} loading="lazy"><figcaption><b>{h}</b>{p}</figcaption></figure>)}<figure class="rv"><img src={`${b}/media/robotics/proto4.webp`} alt="Prototype 3, side view" loading="lazy"><figcaption><b>Prototype 3, side view</b>Cargo box with keypad and LCD on top; servo-mounted ultrasonic sensor at the front.</figcaption></figure></div>
<div class="prose rv" style="margin-top:22px"><h3 class="sub">Notes</h3><p>Power distribution caused most of the problems: the Jetson Nano and the motor driver interfered when they shared a supply. Turning stability depended more on mass than on the controller, and the bias calibration improved straight driving more than PID tuning did.</p></div>
</div>

<div class="wrap section" id="partybot"><hr class="rule"><div class="grid2" style="margin-top:26px">
 <div class="rv"><figure class="fig"><video src={`${b}/media/robotics/partybot.mp4`} poster={`${b}/media/robotics/partybot_poster.webp`} controls preload="none" playsinline></video><figcaption>PartyBot driven from the glove.</figcaption></figure></div>
 <div class="prose rv"><span class="kicker">CENG 424 Embedded Computer Systems · 2026 · with T. Topcu, O. Kansoy, T. Yaraşlı</span><h2 class="h2" style="margin-top:6px">PartyBot: a gesture-controlled mecanum robot</h2>
  <p>Most entertainment props sit in one place and respond to a single button. PartyBot is a mobile platform that the user drives continuously with hand movements.</p>
  <p><b>Wearable controller.</b> A glove carries motion sensors and push buttons on an ESP32. It filters the sensor data, interprets gestures and sends command packets.</p>
  <p><b>Link.</b> ESP-NOW, a low-latency peer-to-peer protocol between the two ESP32 boards, needing no external network.</p>
  <p><b>Robot.</b> A mecanum-wheel base on a second ESP32 that drives, strafes, plays music and shows expressions on a dot-matrix display, with basic fail-safes when the link drops.</p>
  <div class="cta"><a class="btn" href="https://github.com/EmreErturk-3/robotics/blob/main/partybot/presentation.pdf">Slides (PDF)</a><a class="btn" href="https://github.com/EmreErturk-3/robotics/tree/main/partybot">Repository</a></div></div>
</div></div>

<div class="wrap section"><hr class="rule"><div class="prose rv" style="margin-top:26px"><h2 class="h2">Next</h2><p>Both robots use hand-designed behaviours. I am interested in multi-robot systems with behaviour based on local interaction, and in controllers and morphologies obtained by evolution. My reproductions of <a href={`${b}/research/#reproductions`}>BEECLUST and the Vicsek model</a> are a start in that direction.</p></div></div>
</Base>
<style is:global>
.sub{font:500 13px var(--mono);color:var(--mut);margin:0 0 10px;letter-spacing:0}
.protos{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,minmax(0,1fr));gap:16px}.protos img{width:100%;aspect-ratio:4/3;object-fit:cover;border:2px solid var(--wall)}.protos figcaption{font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.45;margin-top:8px}.protos figcaption b{display:block;font:500 13px var(--mono);margin-bottom:3px}
@media(max-width:860px){.protos{grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr))}}
</style>
